problems.problem:
A set $S\subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is given with the properties:
(a) $\mathbb{Z}\subset S$;
(b) $\sqrt 2 +\sqrt 3\in S$;
(c) if $x,y\in S$ then $x+y\in S$;
(d) if $x,y\in S$ then $x y\in S$.
Prove that $(\sqrt 2+\sqrt 3)^{-1}\in S$.

Consider a $7\times 7$ table with entries $a_{ij}\in\{0,1\}$, $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,7\}$. Prove that the entries can be chosen so that the following conditions hold:
(i) each row and each column contains exactly three $1$'s;
(ii) $\sum_{j=1}^7a_{lj}a_{ij}=1$ for all $l,i\in\{1,\ldots,7\}$ with $l\neq i$ (so for any two distinct rows there is exactly one position in which both rows have a $1$);
(iii) $\sum_{i=1}^7a_{ij}a_{ik}=1$ for all $j,k\in\{1,\ldots,7\}$ with $j\neq k$ (so for any two distinct columns there is exactly one position in which both columns have a $1$).

Does there exist a triangle with side lengths $a,b,c$ such that:
(a) $a+b+c=6$, $a^2+b^2+c^2=13$, $a^3+b^3+c^3=28$;
(b) $a+b+c=6$, $a^2+b^2+c^2=13$, $a^3+b^3+c^3=30$?
